Maribor, Slovenia – PRIOT Digital Systems

Getting there:
The most practical option from Ireland would be to fly to Ljubljana Airport and then continue to Maribor by bus, train or shuttle. Slovenia's tourism information confirms that airport transfers include buses and shuttle services. Another option is travelling via Graz Airport in Austria, which has regular connections towards Maribor.

Local transport:
Maribor has both bus and train connections. Maribor Bus Terminal and Železniška postaja Maribor provide useful connections for travelling around Slovenia and to neighbouring cities. Trains connect Maribor with other major Slovenian cities, while buses cover areas not served directly by rail.

For everyday commuting, I would mainly use local buses or walk/cycle, depending on where I find accommodation.

Best option: Bus/walking for daily commuting; train for longer journeys.

🇬🇷 Kavala, Greece – JP IT Solutions & Consulting

Getting there:
The nearest airport is Kavala International Airport "Megas Alexandros", approximately 30 km from Kavala. Αεροδρόμιο Καβάλας «Μέγας Αλέξανδρος» (Kavala Airport “Megas Alexandros”) The airport has bus and taxi connections.

From the airport, public transport connects towards Kavala, although the airport-to-city connection can involve a transfer at the KTEL station in Chrysoupoli.

Local transport:
Kavala has 11 local bus routes, covering areas throughout the city. There are also regional KTEL buses for surrounding towns and beaches.

KTEL of Kavala is the main long-distance bus operator. Kavala does not have a direct railway station; the nearest railway connection is at Drama, around 30 minutes away.

Best option: Local bus for daily travel; taxi when arriving late or carrying luggage.

🇧🇪 Geel, Belgium – BMI Leisure

Getting there:
Geel is well connected to the Belgian railway network. The city has regular train connections on the Antwerp–Neerpelt–Hasselt line.

From Ireland, flying to Brussels Airport would be a practical option. From Brussels Airport, I could take a train towards Geel with one change.

Local transport:
Geel has train and bus connections. De Lijn operates buses and trams throughout Flanders, including connections around Geel.

Cycling would also be a good option for short journeys because Geel is relatively compact and has many student accommodations.

Best option: Train for travelling between cities; bus or bicycle for daily commuting.
